  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild daytime temperatures through Thursday.\n\n-  Patchy valley fog around sunrise each morning.\n\n-  Significant weather change coming Thursday night into Friday.\n\nEnjoy the unseasonable warmth as it will continue through\nThursday. Due to strong valley inversions causing stability, \ntomorrow morning will again give us a chance for patchy valley \nfog. \n\nLate Thursday afternoon an area of showers moves up from the\nsouth. A thunderstorm or two will be possible, especially in\nsouthern Clearwater and western Lemhi Counties, along with Lost\nTrail Pass and potentially as far north as the southern \nBitterroot Valley. This area of showers and thunderstorms will \nbecome just showers after 7-8 pm as afternoon heating quickly \ndissipates. Cloud cover however will keep overnight temperatures \nquite warm for this time of year, with widespread 40-50s as lows, \nexcept along the Continental Divide where overnight temperatures \nmay lower into the 30s. \n\nA strong cold front moves through the region Thursday night into\nFriday, bringing widespread precipitation of up to 0.10 inch but \nsome areas south and east of Missoula could receive up to 0.50 \ninch. Most precipitation will wind down by 6 pm Friday evening. \nOne notable location is Homestake Pass and MacDonald Pass near \nButte, where precipitation may linger between 6 pm and midnight. \nRapidly dropping temperatures could combine with light \nprecipitation to produce a slushy accumulation, leading to the \npotential for a brief period of winter driving conditions at these\nlocations.\n\nThe uncertainty Saturday onward remains in the weather models. The\nNBM forecast has temperatures near normal with a spread of up to 5\ndegrees above or below normal. With a few periods of light\nprecipitation, including the potential of flurries or light snow\nshowers in western MT valleys. More to come as the forecast \nevolves.",
